WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:04.160
By the time it wrapped up, it was agreed that
one of Breaking Bad's critical strengths was

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:08.820
its clear purpose, making it a rare series
without too many plot holes, dropped storylines,

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:10.140
or extraneous scenes.

00:00:10.270 --> 00:00:14.800
The acclaimed 2013 final episode of Breaking
Bad decisively concluded the story of Walter

00:00:14.810 --> 00:00:17.500
White, but the fate of Jesse Pinkman was left
unknown.

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:21.120
Now El Camino: A Breaking Bad Movie is here
to fill in the blanks.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:25.070
Let's delve into the details and dig up some
Easter eggs and references you may have missed.

00:00:25.070 --> 00:00:26.540
And be warned: Spoilers are ahead.

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:32.400
El Camino begins with a flashback of Jesse
and Mike Ehrmantraut making conversation about

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:35.370
where they might escape to if they ever ended
up as fugitives.

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:38.350
Mike is clear in his answer: Alaska, "the
last frontier."

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:42.069
By the time we catch up with Jesse screaming
down the road after the events of Breaking

00:00:42.069 --> 00:00:45.399
Bad, it's clear he's had escape to the great
white north on his mind for a while.

00:00:45.399 --> 00:00:47.940
But first, he has to take care of a few things.

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:52.170
Jesse's dash for freedom is almost immediately
derailed by an army of police officers, alerted

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:55.540
to the white supremacists' compound by the
pounding hellfire of Walt's military-grade

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:56.540
swan song.

00:00:56.870 --> 00:01:00.600
Seeing the red and blue approaching, Jesse
swerves his car into a driveway, laying low

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:02.449
with the lights off as the police pass.

00:01:02.449 --> 00:01:05.700
When they do, he backs out swiftly, knocking
over a mailbox.

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:09.090
The mailbox hangs there, holding in the corner
of the shot as Jesse flees.

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:10.350
The address number: "212."

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:14.830
"Phoenix" is the twelfth episode of Breaking
Bad's second season, or episode 212.

00:01:14.830 --> 00:01:18.570
It's the episode where Jane, Jesse's then-girlfriend,
dies.

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:22.680
It's a turning point in Jesse's life that
takes him from a bad situation to true, prolonged

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:23.220
misery.

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:27.080
Considering that one of El Camino's last scenes
is a flashback to Jesse and Jane, this shoutout

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:28.540
seems suitable.

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:33.299
Over the course of their time on Breaking
Bad, Skinny Pete and Badger prove themselves

00:01:33.299 --> 00:01:34.920
to be surprisingly adorable.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:39.369
They have surprising depth, from Skinny Pete's
showstopping piano skills to the duo's abiding

00:01:39.369 --> 00:01:40.590
passion for sci-fi.

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:44.590
In the season 5 Breaking Bad episode "Blood
Money," Skinny Pete and Badger have an extended

00:01:44.590 --> 00:01:45.840
dialogue about Star Trek.

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:50.080
"So you're telling me every time Kirk went
into the transport, he was killing himself?

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:54.400
So over the whole series there's like 147
Kirks?"

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:55.620
"At least!"

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:59.340
With that background in mind, it's a real
pleasure to see Skinny Pete's house so decked

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:04.140
out in Star Trek paraphernalia, from starships
on the bookshelves to sci-fi inspired chairs

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:05.569
in front of the TV.

00:02:05.569 --> 00:02:09.220
It's just another detail to further flesh
out characters we thought we already knew,

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:13.340
showing how their possibly addictive personalities
can be put toward interests way more wholesome

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:15.040
than what you'd find at the end of a meth
pipe.

00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:19.780
One of the more obscure easter eggs in El
Camino doesn't require an extensive knowledge

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:23.360
of Breaking Bad or its spinoff Better Call
Saul so much as it relies on the viewer's

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:25.820
knowledge of both shows' creator, VInce Gilligan.

00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:29.570
The charming and folksy showrunner has come
to inspire great loyalty in the people that

00:02:29.570 --> 00:02:33.080
he works with, but he also draws similar praise
from fans of his work.

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:36.670
Apart from the quality of his art, that fandom
seems to be partly owed to the fact that he's

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:40.780
proven so willing to engage audiences with
callbacks and references.

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:43.330
In El Camino, one of those nods is a lot more
personal.

00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:46.870
As Todd is driving Jesse to his apartment
to help with an ambiguous, but foreboding

00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:50.700
errand, the car passes by the intersection
of Holly Avenue and Arroz Lane.

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:54.400
Holly, of course, was the name of Walter and
Skyler White's daughter, the newborn whose

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:57.720
future security so motivated Walt's twisted
turn toward crime.

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:01.870
But Holly's name was itself a reference to
a real-life figure: Vince Gilligan's longtime

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:05.080
girlfriend, Holly Rice, since "arroz" is the
Spanish word for "Rice."

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:09.620
Of all people, who would have thought the
Breaking Bad movie would spend so much time

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:10.160
on Todd?

00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:13.660
One of the most loathsome villains in a series
that had plenty to go around, Todd returns

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:18.080
in El Camino in extra-large fashion, with
an extended flashback to a dreadful weekend

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:20.260
Jesse spent in his very weird company.

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:25.620
With his apartment full of serial killer-esque
trophies, sparse decor, and an actual dead

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:31.270
body, Todd in El Camino comes off like a combination
between Jeffrey Dahmer and that kid from St.

00:03:31.270 --> 00:03:31.800
Elsewhere.

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:35.220
The most perplexing objects in Todd's home
can be found in his collection of strange,

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:36.940
apparently hand-made snowglobes.

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:41.480
While they'd be innocuous decor for your average
kindergarten class, they come off as eerie

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:43.230
in the context of Todd's apartment.

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:46.900
The one held up by the faux-police officer
just before a cornered Jesse makes himself

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:51.460
known seems to show a representation of Todd
locked in a winter wonderland with Lydia,

00:03:51.460 --> 00:03:55.360
the high-powered operative in the global meth
scheme whom Todd developed an unsettling fascination

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:55.980
with.

00:03:56.320 --> 00:04:00.320
She is shown literally submerged in a giant
teacup, frolicking while Todd watches.

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:01.060
What a creep.

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:05.640
Breaking Bad loves its motifs, whether it's
a one-eyed pink teddy bear floating in the

00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:09.560
pool or the sky-blue color of methamphetamine,
and so much else.

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:13.870
Another minor motif is dogs, with different
characters being described as dog-like, relating

00:04:13.870 --> 00:04:16.060
to whatever is going on in the story at the
time.

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:20.660
Walt's lab assistant Gale Boetticher is described
euphemistically as a dog who needs to be put

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:22.980
down by Jesse for being an inconvenience.

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:23.980
"He was just like a…

00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:30.420
I don't know, like a... problem dog."

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:34.700
In season five, the unhinged, revenge-focused
Jesse becomes a "rabid dog."

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:39.710
Then, after Walt flees Albuquerque, a single
stray dog crosses the street on the road behind

00:04:39.710 --> 00:04:40.320
him, all alone.

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:44.580
El Camino brings the dog motif back in the
dynamic between Jesse and Todd, who further

00:04:44.590 --> 00:04:47.260
proves his creepiness through the way he treats
his prisoner.

00:04:47.260 --> 00:04:51.400
On multiple occasions, from the hair combing
to remarks about keeping him better groomed,

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:55.060
Todd reveals that he thinks of Jesse as nothing
more than a domesticated animal.

00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:59.010
It's deeply unsettling, and speaks to the
depths of abuse Jesse suffered during his

00:04:59.010 --> 00:04:59.640
captivity.

00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:03.450
Jesse's escape from Albuquerque is an arduous
process.

00:05:03.450 --> 00:05:07.650
When Jesse returns to his parents' home, sneaking
in with a hidden key after luring them off

00:05:07.650 --> 00:05:11.840
of the premises, he makes a beeline to a locked
safe, working to retrieve some old guns for

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:12.840
a coming conflict.

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:16.320
As he makes his way through the house, he
briefly pauses to look up as he's standing

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:19.430
over an evident crawl space outlined in the
floorboards.

00:05:19.430 --> 00:05:22.961
Jesse would have no way of knowing this, but
a crawl space like this one played host to

00:05:22.961 --> 00:05:26.680
one of the most iconic shots in the entire
series, and a low point for Walt.

00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:30.670
In the episode "Crawl Space," Walt ended the
hour literally sunken under ground.

00:05:30.670 --> 00:05:34.720
The famous shot had intense implications for
the story to come: Walt, it seemed, was a

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:35.260
dead man.

00:05:35.260 --> 00:05:38.680
But Jesse's not going to the crawl space,
he's going upstairs, to find a tool for his

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:39.220
freedom.

00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:44.430
Seeing Breaking Bad itself seems like a given
requirement for El Camino, but would viewers

00:05:44.430 --> 00:05:46.680
also have to be caught up on Better Call Saul?

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:48.750
It's a good show, but one easy to sleep on.

00:05:48.750 --> 00:05:51.210
The fourth season isn't even on Netflix yet.

00:05:51.210 --> 00:05:54.330
Vince GIlligan was asked this very question
in an interview with The Hollywood Reporter.

00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:58.310
While he had hopes that the dramatic stakes
would be clear enough to engage new viewers,

00:05:58.310 --> 00:06:00.770
he did agree that it's a better movie for
a fan of the series.

00:06:00.770 --> 00:06:03.040
"By all means, see Breaking Bad first.

00:06:03.220 --> 00:06:04.660
Better Call Saul, not as much."

00:06:04.669 --> 00:06:06.430
You can't say the man lied to you.

00:06:06.430 --> 00:06:10.210
There is indeed at least one Better Call Saul
reference tucked away in El Camino, working

00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:14.200
to further establish this fictional Albuquerque
as a living, breathing place.

00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:17.980
When Jesse arrives for his final confrontation
with Jack and Todd's affiliates, he has to

00:06:17.980 --> 00:06:21.940
wait patiently as the group is entertained
by a group of strippers, brought to the location

00:06:21.940 --> 00:06:23.320
by a bodyguard named Clarence.

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:27.650
This is the same Clarence who proved to be
uniquely unfit for bodyguard duties during

00:06:27.650 --> 00:06:32.280
a previous encounter with Mike in the season
1 episode "Pimento," where he was only referred

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:34.040
to by his size, rather than his name.

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:36.000
"You want one?"

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:43.660
"All right."

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:48.220
When Breaking Bad first started, it was easy
to see Walter White as a good man with his

00:06:48.230 --> 00:06:52.060
back against the wall, and Jesse Pinkman as
his amoral, necessary foil.

00:06:52.060 --> 00:06:53.300
Boy, did that flip by the end.

00:06:53.650 --> 00:06:57.480
One of the ways the audience learned to view
Jesse with compassion was in the way he himself

00:06:57.490 --> 00:07:01.460
was compassionate toward those around him,
particularly children and animals.

00:07:01.460 --> 00:07:05.620
Jesse even treated typically loathsome animals
like creepy-looking insects kindly, as was

00:07:05.620 --> 00:07:07.580
shown in the season two episode "Peekaboo."

00:07:07.580 --> 00:07:11.770
That episode involved Jesse going off on his
most arduous solo mission to date, fending

00:07:11.770 --> 00:07:15.940
off violent, far-gone methheads as he attempted
to also do right by their sorely neglected

00:07:15.940 --> 00:07:16.500
child.

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:20.080
Before that episode took off, though, Jesse
whiled away the time by letting a large black

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:24.580
bug crawl onto the back of his tattooed hand,
regarding it with a smile as it crawled around

00:07:24.580 --> 00:07:25.040
his fingers.

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:28.820
That Skinny Pete then immediately moved to
crush the bug seems to have done little harm

00:07:28.820 --> 00:07:30.120
to their abiding friendship.

00:07:30.340 --> 00:07:34.110
El Camino makes a reference to this scene
as Jesse waits for the strippers to leave

00:07:34.110 --> 00:07:37.740
before his final attack on the welding company
crew, letting a similar bug crawl onto his

00:07:37.740 --> 00:07:38.280
hand.

00:07:38.660 --> 00:07:41.260
He regards it closely this time, keeping his
thoughts private.

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:42.780
At least it's not a housefly.

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:45.280
On Breaking Bad, flies drive you crazy.

00:07:46.470 --> 00:07:50.020
Going into El Camino's mysterious release,
you had to wonder: "Are we going to see any

00:07:50.020 --> 00:07:51.120
Walt in this thing?"

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:54.699
While few really thought the bullet-riddled
Mr. Lambert would be coming back to life,

00:07:54.699 --> 00:07:58.639
it was asking the questions of whether Bryan
Cranston would be back in the role in a flashback

00:07:58.639 --> 00:07:59.639
and a bald cap.

00:07:59.639 --> 00:08:03.669
The answer to both questions turned out to
be "yes" — a somewhat obvious yes, in the

00:08:03.669 --> 00:08:04.919
case of the bald cap.

00:08:04.919 --> 00:08:08.160
From the clothes they're wearing and their
general demeanors, Jesse and Walt's flashback

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:12.300
scene seems to take place immediately after
they've found their way back to civilization

00:08:12.300 --> 00:08:15.460
after being stranded in the season two episode
"4 Days Out."

00:08:15.460 --> 00:08:20.170
The scene features plenty of fanservice, from
Walt absentmindedly forgetting Jesse graduated

00:08:20.170 --> 00:08:21.960
high school to one last, grandiose

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:24.160
"Yeah, b----!"

00:08:24.940 --> 00:08:27.360
During their discussion, Jesse and Walt get
into the mildest of disagreements about the

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:30.840
value of pineapple, which Walt can take or
leave.

00:08:31.460 --> 00:08:35.380
Jesse claims it's good for you, "full of bromine,"
which Walter corrects to "bromelain."

00:08:35.389 --> 00:08:39.650
While Jesse's attempt at trivia is, as Walt
says, not even close to being right, it is

00:08:39.650 --> 00:08:43.420
a reference to the chemical element with forms
the "Br" in Breaking Bad's memorable title

00:08:43.420 --> 00:08:43.960
card.

00:08:45.400 --> 00:08:49.140
When it was first announced as the official
title of the mysterious, shot-in-secret Breaking

00:08:49.140 --> 00:08:52.900
Bad movie, the name El Camino struck many
as only deepening the mystery.

00:08:53.020 --> 00:08:54.019
What could it mean?

00:08:54.019 --> 00:08:58.170
Obviously, the name "El Camino" is most known
as a classic Chevrolet car/truck hybrid, with

00:08:58.170 --> 00:09:01.700
the hood stripes on some models forming the
basis for a few of the Breaking Bad movie's

00:09:01.700 --> 00:09:03.200
promotional posters.

00:09:03.480 --> 00:09:07.640
It's specifically the model of car Jesse uses
to immediately flee from the white supremacist

00:09:07.649 --> 00:09:10.390
camp, but he doesn't hold onto the car for
that long.

00:09:10.390 --> 00:09:13.809
Instead, it remains stowed at Skinny Pete's
place as part of an improvised plan to keep

00:09:13.809 --> 00:09:14.400
Jesse safe.

00:09:14.809 --> 00:09:17.800
After the first fifteen minutes, it's barely
in the movie anymore.

00:09:17.800 --> 00:09:18.800
So what gives?

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:21.660
Breaking Bad was always a series that enjoyed
playing around with titles.

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:25.199
Like in season two, when the titles of each
of the ominous plane crash flashforward episodes

00:09:25.199 --> 00:09:30.590
ultimately spelled out "737 Down Over ABQ,"
or how the last episode's name "Felina" is

00:09:30.590 --> 00:09:35.290
both an anagram of "finale" and a reference
to the Marty Robbins song that scores a critical

00:09:35.290 --> 00:09:36.509
part of the episode.

00:09:36.509 --> 00:09:40.869
Translated into English, "el camino" means
"the way," which is a turn of phrase as open

00:09:40.869 --> 00:09:43.490
to interpretation as Jesse's original fate.

00:09:43.490 --> 00:09:47.339
He spends the movie looking for a way out
of his predicament, and a way forward in his

00:09:47.339 --> 00:09:51.790
life, perhaps ending up, as Aaron Paul told
us so many years ago, as a carpenter living

00:09:51.790 --> 00:09:52.900
quietly in Alaska.

